WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.760
- So I love "A Mighty Win"

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:03.410
I love "Waiting for Guffman".

00:00:03.410 --> 00:00:04.540
But here's my thing,

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:06.560
I laugh the entire time

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:07.393
and I don't

00:00:07.393 --> 00:00:08.226
I know you're an actor

00:00:08.226 --> 00:00:10.060
so like you're supposed
to be able not to laugh.

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:11.590
But how did you not laugh

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:13.610
in all those scenes?

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:15.450
- Well, I,

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:16.460
I did.

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:18.113
(audience laughing)
- [Kelly] You Did?

00:00:18.113 --> 00:00:21.080
- I did, but what particularly,

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:22.350
in "Waiting for Guffman,"

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:24.480
because that was the first one

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.120
and because Chris Guest,

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.500
who I kind of co-wrote the movies with

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:30.550
and Chris directed them

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:32.510
and Chris is one of the funniest guys

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:34.483
ever.
- [Kelly] You're not so bad

00:00:34.483 --> 00:00:35.490
yourself, yeah (laughs)

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:38.200
- Well, he had my number,
let's put it that way.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:41.360
So his character, Corky St.
Clair, was just so funny

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:43.050
that every time he was on camera

00:00:44.844 --> 00:00:46.130
I would laugh.

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:47.360
He would get me laughing.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:51.380
So, there was a group of
us in most of the scenes

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:54.810
and I would move myself behind the group

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:56.750
as the scene was being shot,

00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:59.280
drop to my hands and knees

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.282
and crawl off the set

00:01:01.282 --> 00:01:02.300
(audience laughing)

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:03.900
so nobody would notice

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:07.400
that I was laughing in
the middle of the scene.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.623
And I would virtually disappear.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:11.513
And--

00:01:11.513 --> 00:01:13.040
- 'Cause you don't want
to ruin this scene.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:14.340
- Well that's the thing.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:15.590
- Yeah.

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:18.750
- Because it's improvisational in nature,

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:20.720
you know, what your
doing that's really funny

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:22.563
off the top of your head,

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:25.200
it's really hard to

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:26.033
- Recreate.
- go back

00:01:26.033 --> 00:01:28.320
and recreate that if you laugh

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.450
in the middle of a funny scene.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:31.487
Then they say, "Cut!

00:01:31.487 --> 00:01:33.237
"Okay, boy, that was going so well

00:01:33.237 --> 00:01:35.330
"let's go back and try that again,"

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:36.710
it changes things.

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:38.530
So we learned

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:41.390
it's better not to laugh

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:43.448
no matter how funny you
think something is--

00:01:43.448 --> 00:01:46.050
- That's a talent.
- But in "Waiting for Guffman"

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:48.946
I was crawling off the set
more times than you can count.

00:01:48.946 --> 00:01:49.779
(audience laughing)
- Oh my god, you're like

00:01:49.779 --> 00:01:51.340
that Homer Simpson gif

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:53.437
that just backs up into the bush, like

00:01:53.437 --> 00:01:56.993
like, I just got to not ruin this.

00:01:56.993 --> 00:01:57.826
- That said,

00:01:57.826 --> 00:01:58.680
- That would be so hard!

00:01:58.680 --> 00:01:59.820
- Well, backing up into the bush,

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:01.290
that was like in Guffman.

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:02.600
There was one scene that

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.200
was one of my funniest scenes.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:05.320
It's a group where

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:07.860
we're on stage during our final show

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:09.120
at the end of the movie

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:11.800
and a train is supposed to be pulling away

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:13.350
and what we do is,

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:15.530
we're standing there watching the train

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:17.095
and we kinda go like this.

00:02:17.095 --> 00:02:18.734
- (laughing) No!

00:02:18.734 --> 00:02:19.634
- Yeah, all right.

